1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,033
Stockroom tracks workshop parts, shelf locations and every stock movement.

2
00:00:05,033 --> 00:00:11,433
Search narrows the inventory by item name or SKU.

3
00:00:11,433 --> 00:00:18,800
Stock filters separate low inventory from items that are completely out of stock.

4
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,600
New items start at zero stock. Receipts and issues change the balance through the ledger.

5
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:31,433
A Torx bit set is added with a unique SKU, a shelf location and a reorder threshold.

6
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:34,867
A receipt records both the quantity and its reference.

7
00:00:34,867 --> 00:00:42,233
Receiving five units updates the balance and creates a permanent movement record.

8
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:47,000
The item detail connects its current balance to its transaction history.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,967
Item details can change without rewriting the stock history.

10
00:00:52,967 --> 00:00:57,600
The shelf changes from D-04 to D-05; the five-unit balance stays unchanged.

11
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,433
An issue removes units and records the reason. The resulting balance is shown before saving.

12
00:01:01,433 --> 00:01:08,800
All five units are issued to the workshop, bringing the item back to zero.

13
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:13,533
Only a zero-stock item can be archived. Its receipts and issues remain recorded.

14
00:01:13,533 --> 00:01:17,133
The item is archived after its stock reaches zero.

15
00:01:17,133 --> 00:01:23,600
Archiving removes the item from active inventory without deleting its history.

16
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:29,733
FastAPI publishes the REST endpoints and request schemas through OpenAPI.

17
00:01:29,733 --> 00:01:41,733
POST creates an item, GET reads it, PUT changes its shelf, and DELETE archives it.

18
00:01:41,733 --> 00:01:49,800
PostgreSQL stores the inventory and movement tables; Alembic records the applied migration.

19
00:01:49,800 --> 00:02:05,800
The isolated PostgreSQL test database passes all 21 API and concurrency tests.

20
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:13,967
Build and start the frontend, API and PostgreSQL containers with Docker Compose.

21
00:02:13,967 --> 00:02:19,967
All three long-running Compose services are healthy; the migration job has completed.

22
00:02:19,967 --> 00:02:26,033
Search filters the rows, but the description does not identify the current query.

23
00:02:26,033 --> 00:02:36,100
The inventory description now displays the search term; the browser test checks it.

24
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:46,167
The change and its browser assertion are committed and pushed to main.

25
00:02:46,167 --> 00:02:53,300
Pushing to main starts the test and infrastructure jobs before image publishing.

26
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:59,333
Tests run first; image builds and security scans depend on the earlier jobs passing.

27
00:02:59,333 --> 00:03:05,333
All test, infrastructure, browser and image jobs passed for commit 8a57069.

28
00:03:05,333 --> 00:03:13,367
The published commit passed the pipeline and Trivy HIGH/CRITICAL vulnerability gates.

29
00:03:13,367 --> 00:03:19,200
Both GHCR images use the full commit SHA and run as non-root users.

30
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:23,333
Helm upgrades the local cluster to the exact images published for commit 8a57069.

31
00:03:23,333 --> 00:03:35,200
Helm revision 4 completes successfully, and both Deployments report a successful rollout.

32
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:44,000
The updated description now shows “Search results for Arduino” through the Kubernetes ingress.

33
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,767
The Helm release includes frontend and backend replicas, ClusterIP Services, ingress and an HPA.

34
00:03:51,767 --> 00:03:58,300
Terraform defines the VPC and public/private subnets across two availability zones.

35
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:04,867
EKS enables audit logs and restricts public API access to the configured CIDRs.

36
00:04:04,867 --> 00:04:10,033
The EKS cluster runs in Mumbai.

37
00:04:10,033 --> 00:04:15,167
The managed node group contains two worker nodes.

38
00:04:15,167 --> 00:04:21,300
The API exports request counters and response-time histograms in Prometheus format.

39
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:27,933
The 60-second test completed 10,839 reads with zero failures; the HPA increased backend capacity.

40
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:35,867
Grafana records the traffic increase, zero server errors and scaling from two replicas to five.

41
00:04:35,867 --> 00:04:42,333
Prometheus discovers the backend replicas and successfully scrapes their metrics.

42
00:04:42,333 --> 00:04:51,667
Start two isolated Kubernetes failures: an invalid image and an incorrect Service selector.

43
00:04:51,667 --> 00:05:00,133
ImagePullBackOff identifies the missing image; the Service selector matches no pods.

44
00:05:00,133 --> 00:05:12,200
Correct the image tag and Service selector; the replacement pod becomes Ready and endpoints appear.

45
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:18,367
The AWS plan added 34 resources; the completed teardown removed all 34.

46
00:05:18,367 --> 00:05:26,333
The EKS cluster list is empty after Terraform destroys the environment.
